    "summary": "Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud says UAE support for Somaliland has damaged Somalia's relationship with the Emirates.",
    "content": "Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud has stated that the United Arab Emirates' support for Somaliland has negatively impacted Somalia's relations with the Gulf state. The remarks, reported by Horseed Media, highlight ongoing tensions over the UAE's engagement with the separatist region.\n\nSomaliland declared independence in 1991 but remains unrecognized by the international community, including Somalia, which considers it part of its territory. In recent years, the UAE has deepened its ties with Somaliland through investments in the port of Berbera and a military base, actions that Mogadishu views as undermining its sovereignty.\n\nThe Somali federal government has repeatedly protested what it sees as foreign interference in its internal affairs. President Mohamud's comments underscore the fragility of Somalia's diplomatic relationships when regional authorities pursue independent foreign policies.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant because it affects the political and economic landscape of the Horn of Africa. Many diaspora members have family and business interests in both Somaliland and the rest of Somalia, and strained relations between Mogadishu and Abu Dhabi could impact trade, remittances, and regional stability. The UAE is also home to a large Somali expatriate community, and any diplomatic rift may have implications for their status and livelihoods.\n\nThis latest episode reflects the broader challenge of reconciling Somalia's federal unity with the aspirations of Somaliland and the competing interests of external powers. It remains to be seen how the UAE will respond to President Mohamud's remarks.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/taageerada-imaaraadka-ee-somaliland-oo-sababtay-inuu-xumaado-xiriirkeeda-muqdisho/426231/",
